That's indeed a bit odd. It seems that the conversion tooth-crysknife
deliberately or accidentally drops the 'proofed' bit.

> > No.  If you RTFL on crysknives (i.e. Dune), you see that they are just
> > worm tooths that have been "fixed", and will turn back into worm
> > tooths if they are moved away from humans without special equipment.

Let's look at what NetHack thinks about them.
/n"crysknife":

[The crysknife] is manufactured in two forms from teeth taken
from dead sandworms.  The two forms are "fixed" and "unfixed".
An unfixed knife requires proximity to a human body's
electrical field to prevent disintegration.  Fixed knives
are treated for storage.  All are about 20 centimeters long.
        [ Dune, by Frank Herbert ]

I take it that the knife is not a 'fixed worm tooth'. Rather, 'fixing'
is a treatment applied to the _finished_ knife to make it stable; that
might be a rationalisation for why a fireproof wormtooth doesn't turn
into a fixed crysknife.

> > What this means in Nethack is that when you drop a crysknife it turns
> > back into a worm tooth.
> >
> 
> Stop wielding actually. From weap343.txt: In the special case of
> crysknives, they become "fixed"; unfixed crysknives have a 100% chance
> of reverting to a worm tooth when they no longer held by you or a
> monster, fixed only 10%.

>From checking in debug mode: "no longer held" means "no longer in
inventory (including inside carried containers)". 
A crysknife will stay a crysknife if you unwield it. It will turn into
a tooth if you drop it, or if you drop the container wherein it is
stashed.

